## What Spark does with our data (per privacy policy)

* **Operator entity:** **Spark Mail Limited** (Readdle = parent brand).

* **Auth:** OAuth token (Gmail/Workspace) — **no password stored**.

* **Email content:** originals stay in Google Workspace. Spark servers hold only: notification subject + snippet (encrypted, **deleted \~4h**); Send Later / shared drafts / templates (encrypted, deleted after send).

* **Hosting:** Google Cloud, **US region** (Google Cloud SOC 2 + ISO 27001 certified).

* **Compliance:** GDPR + CCPA; DPO <dpo@sparkmailapp.com>; self-serve "Remove My Data"; **customer DPA published**.

## EU→US transfer — RESOLVED, no server relocation needed

EU data residency was never required (Spark US hosting is fixed/immovable). The US data importer is **Google Cloud, which IS DPF-certified + provides SCCs**; Spark Mail Limited is a non-US "Limited" entity. **Signing Spark's DPA** (Art. 28 + SCC flow-down) + Google's DPF/SCCs makes the transfer lawful as-is. Minor: the main privacy page still cites the dead **Privacy Shield** (a newer Spark KB page already says "Data Privacy Framework") — superseded by the executed DPA; low concern.

## Optional compensating control — shrink the server-side footprint

**Not required at Medium.** Keeping push notifications ON is an accepted choice — the encrypted subject+snippet (deleted \~4h, Google Cloud US) is governed by the DPA + SCCs. If you keep them on, just record "server-side notifications enabled, covered by DPA" in the vendor row. The footprint-reducers below are available only if you want them:

* **Re-add the account declining push notifications at first setup** → Spark "will not send your account information to servers" (also disables Send Later / shared drafts / sync). Cost: no push notifications.

* **Purge existing server data:** Spark Classic → Settings → **Remove My Data From Spark** (or email <dpo@sparkmailapp.com>).

* **Mac per-account:** Settings → Notifications → account → **No Notifications** (reduces snippet syncing).

* Avoid Send Later / shared drafts / templates if footprint must stay minimal.

